      "problem": "Let $A(-2,1,0,-3)$ and $B(1,-1,1,-4).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{266}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
